<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  HelmDeck Wear OS module manifest (W2a). Two facts here are cited from
  developer.android.com/training/wearables/apps/standalone-apps (checked
  2026-08-28), quoted verbatim, not paraphrased from memory:

    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.type.watch" />

  with NO android:required attribute - the doc's own example omits it, and
  the platform default when omitted is required="true". The Wear-OS-
  Machbarkeitsstudie (README.md §6.2) calls out setting required="false" as
  the wrong choice for a real watch app, so this intentionally matches the
  doc's own unqualified form rather than adding an attribute nobody asked
  for.

    <meta-data android:name="com.google.android.wearable.standalone"
               android:value="true" />

  T2 in the study (§6.1/§6.2): the watch talks to the glance-worker over its
  own HTTPS, never through the phone, so it IS standalone by that doc's own
  definition, not merely declared so.

  android:icon / android:roundIcon (added 2026-08-29, the gap the line above
  used to describe). withWearApp.js copies the SAME three brand PNGs app.json
  gives the phone into wear/res/mipmap-xxhdpi and lays down an adaptive-icon
  wrapper - one product, one mark. Until then the module shipped with NO icon:
  it did appear in the drawer, but as a blank system placeholder, so the only
  way to find it was reading the word "HelmDeck".

  2026-08-29 additions (the pairing screen):

    INTERNET - the empty W2a screen made no network call, so it needed none.
    The pairing/claim flow does.

    <queries> for android.speech.RecognitionService - EXACT same trap as the
    phone's withGlassVoice.js already paid for and documented: Android 11+
    hides other packages by default, and ACTION_RECOGNIZE_SPEECH's recognizer
    is IMPLEMENTED BY ANOTHER APP. Without this the APK builds clean and
    `resolveActivity()` finds nothing - "correct code, silent dead
    microphone", not a crash. Copied verbatim (query action + the named
    Google-app package as belt-and-braces), not re-derived, because it is
    already a measured fact in this repo, not a fresh guess for the watch.
    No RECORD_AUDIO here: ACTION_RECOGNIZE_SPEECH launches a SEPARATE
    recognizer activity that captures its own audio in its own process - the
    calling app never touches the microphone directly, so it needs no
    microphone permission of its own (standard Android delegated-intent
    behaviour, not watch-specific).
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!-- W2d (2026-08-29): the watch receives its own sealed FCM push instead of
         relying on the phone being in range to bridge one. POST_NOTIFICATIONS is
         a RUNTIME permission from API 33 on (this watch is API 36) - declaring it
         here is necessary but NOT sufficient, MainActivity must also ask. A
         missing grant makes NotificationManager.notify() do nothing at all, with
         no exception, which is indistinguishable from "push is broken" unless
         someone checks: PushService logs that case rather than failing silently. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <queries>
        <package android:name="com.google.android.googlequicksearchbox" />
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.speech.RecognitionService" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.type.watch" />

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:label="HelmDeck"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault">

        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.android.wearable.standalone"
            android:value="true" />

        <!-- Data-only messages reach onMessageReceived in every app state
             (including killed) ONLY through a registered service - the same
             reason notify.py sends no `notification` block. -->
        <service
            android:name=".PushService"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
